## **19. Data Flow Diagram (Textual)**

### **Slack-Triggered Execution**

```
User types: "@openswe fix authentication bug in <repo:owner/name>"
                                           ↓
                    Slack sends message_events webhook
                                           ↓
                   FastAPI: POST /webhooks/slack
                   (agent/webhooks/slack_routes.py)
                                           ↓
              Parse event, extract thread_ts, channel_id
              Map Slack user ID → GitHub login (via dashboard DB)
              Resolve default repo if not in message
                                           ↓
                   Deterministic thread_id from (channel_ts)
                   Check if thread exists in LangGraph
                                           ↓
           dispatch_agent_run(thread_id, RunInput, config)
           (agent/dispatch.py:_dispatch_input)
                                           ↓
        LangGraph SDK: langgraph_client.runs.create(
            graph_id="agent",
            thread_id=thread_id,
            input=RunInput,
            configurable={...},
            multitask_strategy="interrupt",  # New messages halt active run
            stream_resumable=True,           # Dashboard can replay events
            webhook=COMPLETION_WEBHOOK_URL,  # Called on finish
        )
                                           ↓
            LangGraph API: get_agent(config) factory called
            (agent/server.py:get_agent)
                                           ↓
      Resolve GitHub token for user (OAuth store)
      get_or_create_sandbox(thread_id)
      Resolve model & system prompt + AGENTS.md
      Create Deep Agent with tools + middleware stack
                                           ↓
        Agent loop starts: model.invoke(messages + system_prompt)
                                           ↓
         LLM responds with tool_calls (e.g., ["execute", "slack_thread_reply"])
         Tool dispatch (via Deep Agents)
                                           ↓
            Tool implementations (agent/tools/*.py)
            - execute: Sandbox shell command
            - slack_thread_reply: Post to Slack via API
            - read_file, write_file, etc.: Sandbox file ops
                                           ↓
             Return tool results as ToolMessages
             Middleware intercepts (ToolErrorMiddleware, etc.)
                                           ↓
          Agent loop: Check message queue, inject follow-ups
          Model again with history + tool results
                                           ↓
           [Repeat until model requests no tools or recursion limit hit]
                                           ↓
         LangGraph checkpoints final state, emits completion event
         Webhook notifies FastAPI (COMPLETION_WEBHOOK_URL)
                                           ↓
        Dashboard UI: stream_resumable allows replaying all events
        Slack receives final reply (if slack_thread_reply was called)
```

### **GitHub PR Auto-Review Flow**

```
User opens PR on enabled repo
                ↓
GitHub webhook: POST /webhooks/github (pull_request.opened)
                ↓
Parse PR metadata (owner, repo, PR #, head SHA)
Verify repo is in enabled list + author opted-in
                ↓
Generate thread_id from PR (one per PR, stable)
                ↓
dispatch_agent_run(
    graph_id="reviewer",
    thread_id=pr_thread_id,
    configurable={pr_number, owner, repo, ...}
)
                ↓
Reviewer graph factory (agent/reviewer.py:get_reviewer_agent)
                ↓
Prepare sandbox: deterministic checkout of PR head
Compute unified diff + line ranges
Load review findings schema + guidelines
                ↓
Reviewer Deep Agent with read-only tools:
  - fetch_review_diff (bounded to PR diff)
  - add_finding, update_finding, list_findings
  - publish_review (final step)
  - web_search, fetch_url (read-only research)
                ↓
Model examines diff, emits findings
  (Middleware: RepairOrphanedToolCalls, DynamicContext)
                ↓
Calls publish_review to post GitHub review
                ↓
For each finding thread in GitHub, reconcile with historical threads
                ↓
Set GitHub check run status (COMPLETED / REQUESTED_CHANGES / COMMENTED)
                ↓
(Async) analyzer cron runs nightly:
  dispatch_agent_run(graph_id="analyzer", mode="continual")
  Reads this reviewer's own finding outcomes (resolved/dismissed)
  Refines per-repo review style prompt via save_review_style_prompt
```

